# The web surface talks to the services DIRECTLY, not through the public nginx
# door. Two reasons, both deliberate:
#
#  1. The proxy's rate limiter is keyed on the real client address and loopback
#     is NOT exempt from it — that was the whole point of the trust-proxy fix.
#     Server-side rendering makes several reads per page from one address, so
#     routing them back through the public door would have the site throttling
#     itself and nobody else.
#  2. A server-to-server read is not a public request and should not be counted
#     as one. The numbers in the access log should be visitors.
#
# The browser never calls these. Every page here is rendered on the server, so
# these hosts are never sent to a client.
AGENT_API=http://127.0.0.1:3001
MARKETPLACE_API=http://127.0.0.1:3002
# arca-service runs on 3004 (3003 is taken on the projecteon host). The code
# default, the unit files and the nginx routes all say 3004; a default naming a
# port nothing listens on is how public /v1/arca/* answered 502 unnoticed.
ARCA_API=http://127.0.0.1:3004

# Shown in the footer so a reader can tell which deployment they are looking at.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ARCANA_CHAIN_ID=4663
